# Earth–Moon: two worlds, one moving viewpoint (~2 minutes).
# Inspired by Cosmographia's Earth–Moon example; Cosmolabe syntax.
# Real de440s positions and IAU_MOON orientation, not a mission trajectory.
# Stop at any time in the Script panel and explore; Run restarts the story.
setPlaying off
setTime 2015-10-31T23:50:00Z
setTimeRate 1
clearLookAt
setFrame free-orbit
setLayer axes off
setLayer grid off
setLayer trajectories on
viewpoint "Lunar Orbit"
displayNote "Two worlds, one system. The Moon is about 60 Earth radii away." 6
wait 6

displayNote "Departure: close to Earth, then out toward our nearest neighbor." 5
gotoObject Earth 4
wait 5
dolly 10000 3
wait 3
circleCenterRight 90 4
wait 4

displayNote "Crossing the gap: a camera flight, not a spacecraft trajectory." 6
gotoObject Moon 6
wait 7
setFrame body-fixed Moon
setCamera [5000, 0, 0] [0, 0, 0] [0, 0, 1]
displayNote "The near side faces Earth. The grid and axes reveal the Moon's rotating frame." 6
setLayer axes on
setLayer grid on
wait 6

displayNote "Over the north pole: keep watching the grid as the camera circles." 5
circleCenterUp 90 4
wait 5

displayNote "Around to the far side: the same Moon, a very different view." 6
circleCenterDown 90 3
wait 3
circleCenterRight 180 5
wait 6
setLayer axes off
setLayer grid off

displayNote "Look over the Moon's shoulder toward Earth." 5
crane 2000 3
wait 4
pointAtObject Earth
setPlaying on
setTimeRate 86400
displayNote "One day each second: follow Earth while the background sky changes." 8
wait 8

displayNote "Return to the whole system. Try changing a wait, camera move or time rate and run again." 7
setPlaying off
setTimeRate 1
setTime 2015-10-31T23:50:00Z
clearLookAt
setFrame free-orbit
viewpoint "Lunar Orbit"
wait 7
